{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYpqCKddHvErqNCioBX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Google's Suncatcher prototype, a satellite carrying four TPUs, is online and communicating with the ground after launching this month on a Falcon 9 alongside more than 100 other spacecraft. Engineers spent about three weeks commissioning its subsystems, then sent their first prompt this week, asking the onboard Gemma 4 model for a haiku about being in space. They also asked it to report on the orbital conditions around the satellite, and a Google spokesperson told TechCrunch the satellite is operating nominally.\n\nThe haiku is a demonstration, and the longer test campaign matters more. The satellite generates up to 1 kW, so it can run only one of its four TPUs at a time, for about 20 minutes. Within that limit, Google will look at how the chips handle thermal stress and space radiation. Radiation can cause bit flips that corrupt data without triggering typical monitoring tools. Google has tested the TPUs in particle accelerators on Earth, and those ground tests suggested radiation would have minimal impact on inference and be more of a problem for training. Orbit is the first chance to check that against real conditions rather than a simulation.\n\nIf the chips perform, Google plans to launch two more satellites in 2027 with higher power generation and inter-satellite optical links. Those links are what set Suncatcher apart. It is largely alone among space data center plans in aiming for high-speed connections between close-flying satellites that support coherent distributed workloads. SpaceX's data centers are also expected to use laser links but to orbit too far apart to share processing effectively, and Starcloud and Cowboy Space expect to run inference on individual satellites.\n\nThe economics depend on launch costs. Both Google and SpaceX need Starship to be fully and rapidly reusable, and Suncatcher researchers' peer-reviewed analysis estimated that SpaceX would need to fly Starship 1,800 times to achieve the same learning-driven cost reductions it saw with earlier vehicles. Google is designing for the economics of the 2030s, but the article notes that getting there may depend on how the rest of Alphabet handles near-term AI pressures. Its stock fell this week after it reported that putting AI into search is hurting ad revenue growth."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2715,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2715},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ea27f4b-c52f-4580-a7f5-b3a6b7a08186","uuid":"9b7fd9bc-31b0-419e-9994-dc1d8e5f1466","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:28:49.025Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYppzBPa9u3mWcCkJv8"}
